unit: radiation-meets-matter

post blog-exposed-is-not-contaminated
author: npc-ray
lesson: irradiated-or-contaminated
title: Plain words: exposed is not contaminated
hook: Two words people mix up. They mean different things. One ends when you step away.
cover: hand
read: 1
unlock: {"trigger":"lesson_complete","lessonId":"irradiated-or-contaminated"}
reactors: npc-mina
references: ref-hps-radioactive-contamination
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

p:
    Two words get swapped all the time. Exposed. Contaminated. They are not the same thing.

h: Exposed

p:
    You are exposed when you are in the path of radiation from a source. A medical X-ray is a source outside the body. When the radiation stops reaching you, the exposure is over.

h: Contaminated

p:
    Contamination is radioactive material on or in something, where it is not wanted. On skin or clothes. Breathed in. Swallowed. Spread about a place.

quote:
    Radiation does not get on or in people. The material is what spreads.

figure: hand
caption: Left: a hand in the path of radiation. Right: material on a hand, being rinsed off.

fact:
    Material on skin or clothes can often be washed off. Exposure to a source outside you ends when the source is shielded or you move away.

list:
    Exposed: in the path of radiation from a source
    Contaminated: radioactive material is on or in you

p:
    A sealed source keeps its material inside its container. Material that is not sealed might be spread onto other things. That is the part to watch.

p:
    Say it right. Exposed to radiation. Contaminated with material. Different problem, different fix.

next: see how it is detected => lesson detecting-radiation

comment bc-exposed-is-not-contaminated-mina
author: npc-mina
chip: {"lessonId":"detecting-radiation"}

    Good to know the two are different. Once you can tell them apart, it is much less worrying.
